/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   zs-fixes.css — defects found by LOOKING at the running app.

   Everything in this file comes from a screenshot of the real exe, not from
   reading CSS. That distinction matters: every earlier pass verified computed
   styles, which proves a rule APPLIED and says nothing about whether the
   result is usable. It wasn't.

   Loaded LAST so it wins, and kept in its own file so it cannot collide with
   sheets other work owns.

   ── WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG ────────────────────────────────────────────────

   Measured on the live app, config row width 308px:

       artwork            56px
       "✓ PLAYING NOW"    54px   ← stacked over THREE lines
       chevron button     44px + 8px margin
       gaps/padding      ~24px
       ------------------------
       left for the NAME ~120px

   At 16.5px that truncated the active config to "BO7 - Na…" and its platform
   line to "PlayStati…". The one row the user is currently PLAYING was the one
   they could not read.

   phone.html's own comment says the name "gets a line to itself at 17px
   instead of competing with the platform for one". Three siblings competing
   for the same line is exactly what it was told not to be.

   The fix is to give the width back to the name: the status becomes a compact
   single-line pill, and the chevron keeps its 44px TOUCH target while giving
   up its 44px LAYOUT box. Roughly 40px returns to the name — enough for
   "BO7 - Nametags" at full size.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ── 1. THE ACTIVE-CONFIG STATE PILL ───────────────────────────────────────
   Was a 54px column with the tick on its own line and the words wrapped over
   two more. One line, auto width, and it still says a WORD as well as being
   green — the never-colour-alone rule is not negotiable, so "LIVE" stays. */

.cfg .cstate {
  width: auto !important;
  flex: none;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 5px;
  padding: 3px 9px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  font-size: 10.5px;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: .06em;
  line-height: 1.1;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--green, #3fb950);
  background: rgba(63, 185, 80, .12);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(63, 185, 80, .38);
}
/* The tick stops being a block, which is what forced the third line. */
.cfg .cstate .tick { display: inline; font-size: 11px; line-height: 1; }

/* Replace the two-word label with one that fits, without touching markup or
   any binding key: hide the text node, print a short one. The tick is a child
   element so it survives font-size:0 on the parent. */
.cfg .cstate { font-size: 0; }
.cfg .cstate .tick { font-size: 11px; }
/* "PLAYING", not "LIVE". HOME's badge on the same config says "Playing now"
   and the note at the foot of this list had to name BOTH words to be
   understood — which is the tell that one thing had two names. Same word
   family in both places now, and it still fits on one line. */
.cfg .cstate::after {
  content: "PLAYING";
  font-size: 10.5px;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: .06em;
}
/* It lives INSIDE .cn now (see phone-sections.js's row builder), under the
   identity line rather than in a column beside it, so it costs the config's
   name nothing. align-self keeps it hugging its own words instead of
   stretching across the name column. */
.cfg .cn .cstate { align-self: flex-start; margin-top: 5px; }


/* ── 2. THE CHEVRON KEEPS ITS TOUCH TARGET, NOT ITS FOOTPRINT ──────────────
   44px is the accessibility minimum for a TAP AREA. It is not a requirement
   that the drawn box be 44px wide. .ib already carries an ::after that
   expands the hit region, so widening that lets the visible control shrink
   with no loss of reach — the target stays 44x44, the layout gets 16px back. */

.cfg .ib {
  width: 28px !important;
  height: 44px;
  margin-left: 2px !important;
  border: none;
  background: none;
  box-shadow: none;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: 20px;
}
.cfg .ib::after { inset: -8px -8px -8px -10px; }   /* keeps 44px+ of reach */
.cfg .ib:active { color: var(--accent); }


/* ── 2a. …AND ON THE REORDER SCREEN THAT SAME TRICK OVERLAPPED ─────────────
   Found by arithmetic, not by looking, which is why it survived: the rule
   above is written for the ONE chevron a config row normally carries. In
   reorder mode the row carries TWO keys — ↑ and ↓ — and they inherit it.

       cap 28px · expander +8px right, +10px left · margin-left 2px
       →  hit boxes that OVERLAP BY 16px

   Sixteen pixels of a 46px target belong to BOTH buttons, and the winner is
   whichever ::after paints last. So on the one screen whose entire job is
   moving a row up or down, a user could press "move up" and get "move
   down" — silently, and then it saves. In an accessibility product.

   The expander cannot be shrunk (it is what buys the chevron its 44px), so
   in reorder mode the arrows stop borrowing the chevron's treatment
   altogether and become what they actually are: two real keys, 44x44 with
   no invisible bleed at all, wearing the kit's SECONDARY face so they read
   as pressable rather than as decoration. The row's own flex gap then IS
   the separation — 8px, the touch floor, no expander arithmetic left to get
   wrong. The gap tightens 10px → 8px in this mode only, which also hands
   8px back to the config name the rest of this file fought to widen.

   :has() gates it on "this row has two keys", so the normal row keeps
   section 2 above exactly as it is. Same engine note as section 7: this app
   ships in Chromium only. */

.cfg:has(.ib + .ib) { gap: 8px; }
.cfg:has(.ib + .ib) .ib {
  width: 44px !important;
  height: 44px;
  margin-left: 0 !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);                          /* 6.96:1 on that face */
  font-size: 17px;
}
.cfg:has(.ib + .ib) .ib::after { inset: 0; }       /* the bleed is the bug */
.cfg:has(.ib + .ib) .ib:active { color: var(--text); }
/* Bigger touch targets: the caps grow and so does the space between them —
   the mode is turned on by people who miss, so it must not shrink the gap. */
body.a11y-big .cfg:has(.ib + .ib) { gap: 12px; }
body.a11y-big .cfg:has(.ib + .ib) .ib {
  width: 48px !important; height: 48px; margin-left: 0 !important;
}


/* ── 3. GIVE THE NAME THE ROOM ─────────────────────────────────────────────
   With the two reclaims above, the name column grows by ~40px. It also gets
   permission to use it: min-width:0 is what actually lets a flex child shrink
   its ellipsis boundary correctly rather than overflowing its parent. */

/* Measured after the first round of these fixes: the name was STILL clipped
   ("Halo Infini…" at 88px) because the kit's 14px flex gap costs 42px across
   a four-child row, and the art tile takes 56px. On a 308px row those two
   defaults are a third of the width before a single word is drawn.
   44px art (inline-sized by the renderer, hence !important) + 10px gaps
   returns 30px to the name — with the pill and chevron work above, every
   config name in the current library now fits unclipped at full size. */
.cfg { gap: 10px; }
/* D121 MOVED THIS: the row's picture is WIDE now, 111 x 52, which is 52px
   tall at the shape of Steam's 460 x 215 header, so a game's own header
   fills it with its title whole. The identity line it used to share the
   row with is gone, which is the width that pays for it: see the D121
   section at the end of this file. */
.cfg .art { width: 111px !important; height: 52px !important; border-radius: 10px; }

.cfg .cn { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; }
.cfg .cn .name {
  font-size: 16.5px;
  line-height: 1.22;
  letter-spacing: -.012em;
}
/* The platform line was truncating too ("PlayStati…"). It is secondary, so it
   may shrink a little further rather than clip. */
.cfg .idline .mt { font-size: 12.5px; min-width: 0; }


/* ── 4. THE DEVICE BAR'S EMPTY STATE ───────────────────────────────────────
   With the board offline it rendered as two lines of plain grey text on a
   slab — indistinguishable from a screen that failed to load. It is a normal,
   expected state and should look deliberate: one line, centred, with the
   reticle's idle language rather than a sentence fragment. */

.dvb-empty, .dvb-idle {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 7px;
  padding: 2px 4px;
  font-size: 11.5px;
  opacity: .8;
}
.dvb-empty::before, .dvb-idle::before {
  content: "";
  width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--muted);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(134, 149, 168, .13);
  flex: none;
}
/* Two stacked "nothing is happening" lines is one line too many. */
.devbar .dvb-row + .dvb-row:has(.dvb-idle) { display: none; }


/* ── 5. THE MARK ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   At 26px the reticle's ring, ticks and crosshair collapse into noise around
   the ZS. It is the product's signature; give it the few pixels that make it
   legible. */

.appbar h1 .brandmark { height: 30px; }


/* ── 6. THE SECTION HEADER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   "YOUR CONFIGS — TAP TO SWITCH" is an instruction shouted in caps at the top
   of the screen every time. The tap affordance is the row; the header should
   name the thing. Shortened without touching markup, so nothing that reads
   the DOM changes. */

.home .grp { font-size: 11px; }


/* ── 7. THE MARK SHOWN TWICE PER ROW ───────────────────────────────────────
   Seen in the screenshot: the 56px art tile AND the small idline badge were
   the SAME family mark, twice, 12px apart, on every row.

   The rule in phone.html — "the family mark is ALWAYS here, even when a photo
   is set" — exists so a PHOTO is never the only identity on a row. That rule
   is right and this does not touch it: when .art contains an <img> (a photo),
   the badge stays. Only when .art contains an <svg> — i.e. the tile already
   IS the family mark — is the badge a literal duplicate, and only then does
   it go. :has() is fine here; this app ships in Chromium only. */

.cfg:has(.art > svg) .idline .mk { display: none; }


/* ── ACCESSIBILITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   High contrast: the pill's tinted background is the first thing to go, since
   it is decoration; the border and the word carry the meaning. */

@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  .cfg .cstate { background: none; box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px currentColor; }
  .cfg .ib { color: var(--text); }
}
body.a11y-contrast .cfg .cstate {
  background: none;
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px currentColor;
}

/* Bigger touch targets mode must still get a real box back. */
body.a11y-big .cfg .ib { width: 40px !important; margin-left: 6px !important; }


/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   COLOUR THE ROOMS — 2026-08-18
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   Everything below comes from measuring the RUNNING app at 375×812 through
   Chrome's debugger: 41 frames, decoded to pixels, distinct painted surfaces
   counted at ≥1% of the viewport, and "flat" = the largest CONNECTED run of
   one identical colour. Three numbers ran the whole pass:

     · SOLID RED APPEARED ON ZERO SCREENS.       0.00% of #cf3f45, everywhere.
     · SOLID PRIMARY APPEARED ON 3 OF 25.        The other 21 had 0.07–0.47%
       ion — an icon chip and a status dot, not a button.
     · ON 22 OF 31 SCREENS THE MOST PROMINENT PAINTED ELEMENT WAS div.phead —
       the BACK BUTTON. App-wide, 28.6% of the viewport was one flat colour
       and 6.3% of pixels had any chroma at all. Every dominant surface
       (#06080c #121824 #1a202c #1e242f #212632 #232833 #27313f) sat at hue
       215–220°, so the owner's "too much dark blue on blue" was literally
       true: 94% of the app was ONE HUE.

   The system to fix it already existed — the four button roles in phone.html
   §2 and the five section tints in zs-kit.css §17. It simply was not reaching
   the screens. This file is the reach.

   ONE RULE IS NOT TOUCHED, deliberately, and it is worth naming because the
   brief asked for it: FOCUS RINGS STAY ONE COLOUR. zs-kit §17 forbids tinting
   them ("one colour everywhere or keyboard users lose the thread") and that is
   an accessibility decision on an accessibility product, not a style. Rooms
   get headers, ticks, rails, selection and switches. They do not get the ring.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ── 8. THE ROOM YOU ARE STANDING IN, ON EVERY SURFACE OF IT ───────────────
   data-tint has been on #dbody since 2026-08-17 and it reached six things.
   These are the rest: the eyebrow itself, the hairline that trails it, the
   header above the screen and the chip that leaves it — so a glance anywhere
   on the frame answers "which room" without reading a word. The tint values
   themselves live in zs-kit.css §17 (Keybinds moved off grey onto M&K violet,
   Vision off Overview's ion onto its own gradient's blue, and Global Settings
   joined on neutral steel). */

[data-tint] .sh, [data-tint] .grp, [data-tint] .pkh { color: var(--tint); }
/* the trailing hairline joins the bracket it started as
     ion 10.10:1 · gold 9.13:1 · violet 5.96:1 · orange 8.14:1
     periwinkle 6.79:1 · steel 10.27:1 — all past AA on the page */
[data-tint] .sh::after, [data-tint] .pkh::after {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--tint-edge), transparent);
}

/* THE SKY. The cheapest possible answer to a 30–53% flat expanse: the room's
   own wash, ~13% alpha, fading out over the first 320px of CONTENT and the
   last 220px of it. `local` — not the default `scroll` — is the load-bearing
   word: it attaches the gradient to the scrolled content, so this is the top
   and the bottom of the SCREEN rather than a permanent colour cast that
   follows you down a long list. At 13% over #06080c the ground moves to about
   #0d1420; --text goes 17.05:1 → 16.2:1 and --muted 7.85:1 → 7.5:1, so
   nothing on any of these screens loses AA to it. */
#dbody[data-tint] {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--tint-wash), transparent 320px),
    linear-gradient(0deg, var(--tint-wash), transparent 220px);
  background-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
  background-attachment: local, local;
}

/* THE HEADER AND THE WAY OUT.
   div.phead was the most prominent painted element on 22 of 31 screens — the
   loudest thing in two-thirds of this product was the back button — and
   .pback's ink was --accent, i.e. brand furniture wearing the same ion that
   is supposed to mean "press this and the thing happens". Both now follow the
   room: a 3px rail under the header and the chip's ink in the room's hue. The
   header is a sibling of #dbody, not an ancestor, so :has() is how the
   attribute is reached — the same Chromium-only assumption sections 2a and 7
   above already make. Ion is NOT freed on Overview (its tint IS ion) and does
   not need to be: the point was never that the chip is cyan, it was that cyan
   was the only chroma on the frame. */
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="overview"]) .phead { --rail: var(--accent, #6f9bff); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="aim"])      .phead { --rail: var(--gold); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="bindings"]) .phead { --rail: var(--purple); }
/* Smart Actions and Auto Actions share the rose (D127); Auto Actions had no
   line here at all, so its header wore no rail and a white back chevron */
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="smart"])    .phead { --rail: var(--rose, #f47fa4); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="auto"])     .phead { --rail: var(--rose, #f47fa4); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="vision"])   .phead { --rail: var(--tint-vision, #5ccfd6); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint="global"])   .phead { --rail: var(--steel, #b9c6d8); }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint]) .phead {
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--rail);
  border-bottom-color: transparent;
}
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint]) .phead .pback { color: var(--rail); }


/* ── 9. THE DESTRUCTIVE ROLE, WHERE THE ROLE IS A GROUP ────────────────────
   Overview / Advanced measured 38.3% flat with five accordion doors, and
   "Danger zone — nothing in here can be undone" wore the IDENTICAL teal chip
   as "More board details — numbers for the curious". The screen said in words
   that one of them was irreversible and said in colour that they were the
   same thing. acc() already stamps data-key, so the row can be reached
   without a renderer change. Global Settings' own "DANGER ZONE" header is the
   .sh.danger half of the same fix. */

.acc[data-key="danger"] {
  border-color: rgba(207, 63, 69, .48);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
}
.acc[data-key="danger"] > .ah { color: var(--red-ink); }        /* 5.43:1 on a card */
.acc[data-key="danger"] > .ah .aicon,
.acc[data-key="danger"].open > .ah .aicon {
  background: rgba(207, 63, 69, .17);
  border-color: rgba(255, 128, 120, .58);
  color: var(--red-ink);
}
.acc[data-key="danger"] > .ah .chev { color: var(--red-ink); }

/* the group header for the same job — Global / Developer's "Danger zone",
   which was printing a teal bracket two rows under an "Inspect" that had the
   same one. #ff8078 measures 7.31:1 on the page. */
.sh.danger { color: var(--red-ink); }
.sh.danger::before { border-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45); }
.sh.danger::after {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(207, 63, 69, .8), transparent);
}


/* ── 10. THE ONE ACTION PER SCREEN, WHEN THAT ACTION IS A ROW ──────────────
   The Aim screen is the best-tinted screen in the app and it still had no
   solid primary — nothing on it said "start here". It does have the right
   candidate already: Match Your Game, which writes speed, curve and smoothing
   from a preset and is the single highest-value thing a new owner can press.
   It was a grey door row identical to the four others around it.

   A NAVIGATING PRIMARY IS NOT A NEW IDEA IN THIS APP — HOME's "Edit this
   config" rail is exactly that, a full-width solid ion row that opens a
   screen. This is the same role, drawn the same way, so pressing it teaches
   the same lesson. door()'s `kind` argument gates it: at most one per screen.
     --btn-1-ink #04120f on the cap 10.87:1 mid, 8.82:1 at its foot
     the hint at .78 alpha over the cap measures 5.7:1 */

.grp-card:has(> .lrow.tapper.go) {
  padding: 0; border: none; background: none; box-shadow: none; overflow: visible;
}
.lrow.tapper.go {
  border: 1px solid var(--btn-1-edge, #a8c4ff);
  border-radius: var(--r-card, 14px);
  min-height: 60px;
  background-color: var(--btn-1-bg, #6f9bff);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 100%);
  color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.42), 0 3px 0 var(--btn-1-wall, #123a6b),
              0 8px 20px rgba(111, 155, 255,.22);
}
.lrow.tapper.go .src { color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f); font-weight: 700; }
/* beats the inline color:var(--muted) door() writes — a grey hint on a cyan
   cap measures 1.9:1, which is the same defect as #fff on the accent */
.lrow.tapper.go .src em { color: var(--cap-ink-soft) !important; }
.lrow.tapper.go > .ico, .lrow.tapper.go .arrow, .lrow.tapper.go .rt {
  color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f);
}
.lrow.tapper.go:active, .lrow.tapper.go.zs-pressing {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
}
/* high contrast keeps the fill, exactly as .btn.primary does */
body.a11y-contrast .lrow.tapper.go { border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none; }


/* ── 11. THE CONFIGS SCREEN'S OWN ACTION ───────────────────────────────────
   ＋ New config sat below fifteen rows AND below the paragraph under them, in
   the CREATE role's dashed empty socket — right for "add a step to a list",
   wrong for the one thing on this screen that is not "tap a row". It has moved
   directly under the header (phone-sections.js) and takes the solid ion face
   here, keeping BOTH its id (four gates select on it) and its .newcfg class
   (emulate-app.js selects on that).

   THIS IS THE THIRD ELEMENT IN THE APP ALLOWED A SOLID ION FILL, and the rule
   it joins is unchanged in substance: zs-kit §7 reserves the surface for "press
   this and something happens" and forbids SELECTION (tabs, segments) from
   borrowing it. Creating a config is the former. The count was never the
   point; the prohibition was. */
.newcfg#newcfg {
  margin: 2px 0 10px;
  min-height: 52px;
  border: 1px solid var(--btn-1-edge, #a8c4ff);
  border-style: solid;
  background-color: var(--btn-1-bg, #6f9bff);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 100%);
  color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f);
  font-weight: 700;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.42), 0 3px 0 var(--btn-1-wall, #123a6b),
              0 8px 20px rgba(111, 155, 255,.22);
}
.newcfg#newcfg:active, .newcfg#newcfg.zs-pressing {
  filter: none;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
}
body.a11y-contrast .newcfg#newcfg { border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none; }


/* ── 12. THE KILL SWITCH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Overview / Now's most prominent element was the DualSense picture at 33.9%
   of the viewport, and the master "Adapter is sending inputs" switch — the
   only control on that screen a panicking user needs — was BELOW THE FOLD.
   The renderer now puts the switch first (phone-sections.js). This makes it
   look like what it is.

   THE ONLY RED-WHEN-OFF CONTROL IN THE APP, and that is on purpose: every
   other switch's off state is a preference, this one's is "the adapter has
   stopped pressing anything". Colour is not the only signal — the knob still
   travels, .sw.on still exists for the accessibility tree, and the sub-label
   still says which state is which; the rail is a fourth channel on the one
   control that has earned four. :has() reads the switch's own class, so the
   card follows a tap with no JS and the ~8s poll never has to repaint it. */
.killcard { position: relative; overflow: hidden; }
.killcard::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0; width: 4px;
  background: var(--accent, #6f9bff);
}
.killcard > #ov-kill > .fld {
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(111, 155, 255,.13), transparent 72%);
}
.killcard > #ov-kill .flab { font-weight: 700; }
/* OFF — the emergency state, and it says so in red as well as in words */
.killcard:has(#ov-kill .sw:not(.on))::before { background: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45); }
.killcard:has(#ov-kill .sw:not(.on)) > #ov-kill > .fld {
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(207,63,69,.20), transparent 72%);
}
.killcard:has(#ov-kill .sw:not(.on)) > #ov-kill .flab { color: var(--red-ink); }


/* ── 13. GLOBAL / PERFORMANCE — THREE BIG NUMBERS ──────────────────────────
   Least colourful screen in the product (meanChroma 11.5), 42.7% flat, no
   primary, no red, and it does not scroll — so that WAS the whole screen. Its
   only living content was three 13px values pinned to the right edge of three
   identical rows. At 26px tabular they are readable across a room, they fill
   the dead half of the pane with the one thing on it that moves, and every
   label is the word it always was.
     --white on a card 17.77:1 · the tint carries the number on rooms that
     have one; on Global that is --steel at 10.27:1. */
.grp-card.bigstats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; padding: 0; overflow: hidden; }
.bigstats > .bigstat { padding: 15px 16px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 3px; min-width: 0; }
.bigstats > .bigstat:nth-child(1) { grid-column: 1 / -1; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
.bigstats > .bigstat:nth-child(3) { border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
.bigstat b {
  font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -.022em;
  color: var(--white, #fff);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1;
  overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
[data-tint] .bigstat b { color: var(--tint); }
.bigstat b i { font-style: normal; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--muted); }
.bigstat span {
  font-size: 10.5px; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: .07em; color: var(--muted);
}


/* ── 14. COMPUTER VISION / TUNE — THE MODE YOU ARE IN, LOUDLY ──────────────
   41.4% flat, no primary, and the two things you can actually do here — Pick a
   colour and Draw boxes — were grey outline chips indistinguishable from every
   other control in the app. They are a MODE SELECTOR, not two actions, so the
   honest fix is not a primary (zs-kit §8 forbids selection wearing solid ion,
   and it is right to): it is making the selected mode a FILLED chip, so the
   screen says which of its two tools is in your hand.
   It used to fill in the ROOM's colour. It carries an id, so it out-ranked
   §16 and would have been the one selection left in the app painting a
   per-room hue — the whole point of §16 being that "selected" is one colour
   everywhere. Same deep accent, same numbers, same boundary. */
#cv-modeseg button.on {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: var(--sel, #2f4c8f);
  border-color: var(--accent, #6f9bff);
  color: var(--sel-ink, #ffffff);
  font-weight: 700;
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, .30), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.22);
}
#cv-modeseg button.on:active { box-shadow: inset 0 2px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, .34); }
body.a11y-contrast #cv-modeseg button.on { border: 2px solid var(--text); }
@media (prefers-contrast: more) { #cv-modeseg button.on { border: 2px solid var(--text); } }


/* ── 15. THE NEW-CONFIG WIZARD — THE WORST SCREEN IN THE APP ───────────────
   Step 1 of 4 measured 53.3% of the viewport as ONE FLAT #06080c, four painted
   surfaces (the fewest anywhere), 3.0% chromatic pixels, no primary, no red,
   and the most prominent element was the back button. The first thing a new
   owner ever does was the emptiest screen we ship — and the three tiles that
   DID carry real family colour spent it on a 46px badge floating on identical
   grey, in a 2+1 grid whose third cell was a hole.

   The family colour becomes the ROW. Same meaning it has everywhere else in
   the product (docs/DESIGN.md: blue = PlayStation, green = Xbox, violet =
   keyboard & mouse), so a colour learned here is worth something on the next
   screen. One column, so no hole. And a step rail above it, which is the
   wizard's own state and the note under it already says the same thing in
   words — the rail is a second channel, never the only one. */

.wzrail { display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 4px 2px 12px; }
.wzpip {
  flex: none; width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  color: var(--muted);
  background: var(--zs-control, #27313f);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge, #7387a3);
}
.wzpip.done {                                   /* --accent on the wash 8.9:1 */
  background: rgba(111, 155, 255, .18);
  border-color: rgba(111, 155, 255, .55);
  color: var(--accent, #6f9bff);
}
.wzpip.now {                                    /* --btn-1-ink on ion 10.87:1 */
  background: var(--btn-1-bg, #6f9bff);
  border-color: var(--btn-1-edge, #a8c4ff);
  color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 0 var(--btn-1-wall, #123a6b), 0 0 14px rgba(111, 155, 255, .30);
}
.wzbar { flex: 1 1 auto; height: 3px; background: var(--zs-control, #27313f); }
.wzbar.done { background: var(--accent, #6f9bff); }

.wzrows { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 10px; margin-top: 6px; }

/* THE WIZARD KEEPS ITS OWN SKY. The five rooms get theirs from data-tint;
   this screen is deliberately untinted (it belongs to no config yet) and was
   still measuring 50% flat #06080c after the rows landed — three rows and a
   note cannot fill 812px on their own. #dbody carries data-ns, and the wizard's
   is a constant, so the one screen that needs it can have it without a
   renderer change and without tinting the Configs list beside it. Ion,
   because the wizard's own step rail is ion: the screen has one colour and it
   is the colour of "you are creating something". */
#dbody[data-ns="global/new"] {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(111, 155, 255,.10), transparent 300px),
    linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(111, 155, 255,.07), transparent 260px);
  background-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
  background-attachment: local, local;
}
@media (prefers-contrast: more) { #dbody[data-ns="global/new"] { background-image: none; } }
body.a11y-contrast #dbody[data-ns="global/new"] { background-image: none; }

.tile.fam {
  --fam: var(--steel, #b9c6d8);
  --famw: rgba(185, 198, 216, .11);
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 52px minmax(0, 1fr) 16px;
  grid-template-rows: auto auto;
  column-gap: 12px;
  align-items: center;
  text-align: left;
  padding: 18px 12px;
  min-height: 88px;
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-left: 4px solid var(--fam);
  background-color: var(--panel);
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--famw), transparent 68%);
}
/* the hues are the family set, used FOR their meaning. "PC" takes violet
   because marks.js already draws its badge from the mkb family — the row and
   the badge on it must not disagree. */
.tile.fam[data-fam="ps5"]  { --fam: var(--blue, #58a6ff);   --famw: rgba(88, 166, 255, .17); }
.tile.fam[data-fam="xbox"] { --fam: var(--green, #3fb950);  --famw: rgba(63, 185, 80, .17); }
.tile.fam[data-fam="pc"],
.tile.fam[data-fam="mnk"]  { --fam: var(--purple, #a371f7); --famw: rgba(163, 113, 247, .19); }
.tile.fam[data-fam="controller"] { --fam: var(--steel, #b9c6d8); --famw: rgba(185, 198, 216, .14); }

.tile.fam > .tart { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1 / 3; height: auto; width: 52px; }
.tile.fam > div:nth-of-type(2) {
  grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; margin-top: 0 !important;
  font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: 650;
}
.tile.fam > div:nth-of-type(3) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 2; margin-top: 2px !important; }
/* D399: a row with a small second line under its name (the PlayStation 5
   row's "P5General dongle needed"): name, that line, then the description,
   with the picture and the chevron spanning all three */
.tile.fam.has-sub { grid-template-rows: auto auto auto; }
.tile.fam.has-sub > .tart,
.tile.fam.has-sub > .wzgo { grid-row: 1 / 4; }
.tile.fam.has-sub > div:nth-of-type(3) { margin-top: 1px !important; }
.tile.fam.has-sub > div:nth-of-type(4) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 3; margin-top: 4px !important; }
.tile.fam > .wzgo {
  grid-column: 3; grid-row: 1 / 3; justify-self: end;
  color: var(--fam); font-size: 20px; line-height: 1;
}
/* Selected keeps all three of the kit's channels — 2px edge, wash, corner
   tick — and simply swaps ion for the family, because on this screen the
   family IS what was chosen. */
.tile.fam.on {
  padding: 17px 11px;
  border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--fam);
  border-left-width: 4px;
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--famw), transparent 68%);
}
.tile.fam.on::after { color: var(--fam); right: 26px; }


/* ── ACCESSIBILITY, RESTATED FOR THE NEW SURFACES ──────────────────────────
   Same trade §14 of the kit already makes: washes and gradients go, the
   strong indicators (edges, rails, the family colour itself) stay. */

@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  #dbody[data-tint] { background-image: none; }
  .tile.fam { background-image: none; border-left-width: 5px; }
  .tile.fam.on { border-width: 3px; border-left-width: 5px; background-image: none; }
  .killcard > #ov-kill > .fld,
  .killcard:has(#ov-kill .sw:not(.on)) > #ov-kill > .fld { background-image: none; }
  .lrow.tapper.go { background-image: none; }
}
body.a11y-contrast #dbody[data-tint] { background-image: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .tile.fam { background-image: none; border-left-width: 5px; }
body.a11y-contrast .tile.fam.on { border-width: 3px; border-left-width: 5px; background-image: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .killcard > #ov-kill > .fld,
body.a11y-contrast .killcard:has(#ov-kill .sw:not(.on)) > #ov-kill > .fld { background-image: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .lrow.tapper.go { background-image: none; }

@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
  #dbody[data-tint] { background-image: none; }
  .tile.fam { background-image: none; }
  .killcard > #ov-kill > .fld { background-image: none; }
}


/* ── 16. THE ROOM COLOURS THE PLACE. THE ACCENT COLOURS THE ACTION ────────
   THIS SECTION USED TO SAY "the room's colour reaches the whole room", and
   it did reach it — which is the defect the owner named: "it's a cluttered
   confusing mess". The measurement that motivated it was real (94% of
   painted pixels on one hue, no solid role fill on eleven of twenty
   screens) and the fix for it was half right. What went wrong is WHICH
   HALF of each screen took the room's colour.

   Painting the FURNITURE in the room's hue is right — the eyebrow, the
   header rail, the card veil, the icon chips. A glance anywhere answers
   "which room am I in" without reading a word, and none of it is a control.

   Painting the CONTROLS in the room's hue is what broke the app. Measured
   across four screens, the identical segmented control painted a different
   "selected" on each one: Overview teal rgb(26,112,109) · Aim gold
   rgb(227,179,65) · Smart Actions orange rgb(255,148,87) · Screen Assist
   blue rgb(111,155,255). Same widget, same meaning, four colours — so the
   one state a user's eye needs to read fastest, "which of these is on",
   had to be re-learnt in every room. The switch, the slider thumb, the
   +/− keys, the create button and the live readouts all did the same. Six
   rooms, six vocabularies for ON.

   SO THE LINE IS DRAWN AT MEANING, NOT AT SURFACE.
     the room's hue  →  where you ARE      (eyebrow, rail, card veil, chip)
     the accent      →  what you DID       (selected, on, the thing you drag)
     --muted         →  what is furniture  (chevrons and arrows: five
                                            coloured arrows saying "this is
                                            a door" when every row is a door)
   Colour stays a redundant channel throughout: every selected segment is
   also bold, also raised, also rail-marked; every switch also travels.

   THE SELECTION IS A DEEP ACCENT, NOT THE BRIGHT CAP. zs-kit §8's rule —
   a solid bright accent surface means "press this and the thing happens",
   and nothing else — still stands, and it is why this cannot simply be
   #6f9bff. It is the accent mixed halfway into the page, which is a
   different material from the cap by 3.06:1, with the accent itself as its
   boundary. That is the same argument the old code already made for
   Overview alone (#1a706d); all six rooms now make it, in one hue.
     white on #2f4c8f          8.23:1  (AA, and AAA at the pill's weight)
     #2f4c8f against the cap   3.06:1  (WCAG 1.4.11 — not the primary)
     the #6f9bff border        6.61:1 against a card — the real boundary */

/* FURNITURE GOES QUIET. These three were re-pointed at the room by the
   previous pass because they were hard-coded ion inside a gold screen. They
   are chevrons and carousel arrows on rows and cards that already say in
   words what they do, so the answer was never "make them gold" — it was
   that a decoration should not be wearing a signal's clothes at all.
   --muted measures 6.0:1 on a card: legible, and silent. */
[data-tint] .lrow .arrow,
[data-tint] .smcar .arr,
[data-tint] .card .ci { color: var(--muted); }

/* A CONTROL IS THE SAME CONTROL IN EVERY ROOM. The create socket, the
   stepper keys and the live readout beside a slider are things you press or
   change, so they take the accent — the one colour in the app that means
   exactly that — instead of a sixth per-room variant of it. */
[data-tint] .addbtn, [data-tint] .newcfg {
  color: var(--accent);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 62%, transparent);
}
[data-tint] .addbtn:active, [data-tint] .newcfg:active {
  border-color: var(--accent);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint, rgba(111, 155, 255,.14));
}
[data-tint] .stp button {
  color: var(--accent);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 15%, transparent),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent));
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line, rgba(111, 155, 255,.55));
}
[data-tint] .flab .rv { color: var(--accent); }

/* THE ON SWITCH AND THE THING YOU DRAG. Both were re-inked per room by
   zs-kit §17; both are state, so both come back to the accent. The knob's
   travel and the thumb's knurl are untouched — position and texture are the
   channels that survive colour being removed entirely. */
body [data-tint] .sw.on {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint, rgba(111, 155, 255,.28));
  border-color: var(--accent);
}
body [data-tint] .sw.on::after {
  background: var(--accent);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.45), var(--el-1);
}
[data-tint] input[type=range].rng::-webkit-slider-thumb {
  background:
    repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0,0,0,.22) 0 1px, transparent 1px 3px),
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--accent), var(--accent));
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--bg), 0 0 0 4px var(--zs2-ion-line, rgba(111, 155, 255,.55)),
              0 3px 7px rgba(0,0,0,.65), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.5);
}
[data-tint] input[type=range].rng::-moz-range-thumb {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--accent), var(--accent));
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--bg), 0 3px 7px rgba(0,0,0,.65);
}

/* ONE "SELECTED", APP-WIDE. Six rules became one: the two the previous pass
   shipped (a bright room-hue fill for five rooms, a deep teal for Overview)
   are both gone, and #edtabs joins so the editor's own tab strip cannot
   drift either. Written at the same specificity as the rules it replaces
   and later in the file, which is how it wins. */
body [data-tint] .seg2 button.on,
body [data-tint] .subtabs button.on,
body [data-tint] #edtabs button.on {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: var(--sel, #2f4c8f);
  border-color: var(--accent);
  color: var(--sel-ink, #ffffff);
  font-weight: 700;
  text-shadow: none;
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.30), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.22);
}
body [data-tint] .seg2 button.on:active,
body [data-tint] .subtabs button.on:active,
body [data-tint] #edtabs button.on:active {
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.34);
}

/* THE CARD JOINS THE ROOM. A 7% veil of the room's own hue over the card
   ladder — not a new surface, the same one wearing the room's light. This is
   the rule that answers "94% of pixels are one hue": on a screen that is half
   cards, half its pixels stop being neutral without a single control changing
   colour. Luminance moves under 2%, so every measured pair in zs-kit §5
   survives (--text on the veiled gold card 16.8:1, --muted 7.4:1). The tint's
   own left rail then turns a stack of cards into a spine. */
[data-tint] .grp-card,
[data-tint] .acc,
[data-tint] .vizbox {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(0deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 7%, transparent),
                          color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 7%, transparent)),
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
}
/* the spine. Three exclusions, each measured rather than guessed:
     · the danger accordion — §9 owns that edge and it is red
     · a card dissolved to carry a solid primary row (§10) — re-adding a
       border there would draw a box round the cap
     · A CARD OF LIST ROWS. The rows carry the room colour themselves (§17),
       and with both drawn the card's rail sat in the same 3px column and
       hid every row's state behind one unbroken bar — the exact defect §17
       exists to fix. Where rows speak, the card is quiet. */
[data-tint] .grp-card:not(:has(> .lrow.tapper.go)):not(:has(> .lrow)),
[data-tint] .acc:not([data-key="danger"]) {
  border-left: 3px solid var(--tint-edge);
}

/* THE BACK HEADER STOPS BEING THE LOUDEST THING (the audit: div.phead was the
   most prominent painted element on 22 of 31 screens). §8 gave it the room's
   rail and the room's ink; what was left making it shout is that the chip is
   the only RAISED control on the frame — a full control-face gradient plus a
   sheen, in the top-left corner where the eye lands first. It keeps its 44px
   target and its border (it must still read as pressable — a deliberate
   earlier fix) and gives up the lift: flat page-level fill, no sheen, so the
   screen's own content wins the frame. */
.phead { box-shadow: none; }
#detail:has(#dbody[data-tint]) .phead { box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--rail); }
.pback {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: var(--zs-page, #06080c);
  box-shadow: none;
}


/* ── 17. KEYBINDS — 4483px OF IDENTICAL SLABS GETS A STRUCTURE ─────────────
   The flagship of the eleven: 6.2 screenfuls, ~36 rows, ZERO solid role fill
   anywhere in the scroll extent, and every row painted the same. The violet
   was in the top wash and one group tick and nowhere else. Three separate
   defects, three fixes, and not one of them is "add colour":

   1. THE ONE ACTION WAS AT THE BOTTOM. "＋ Add a button" (#sa-addinput) sat
      below thirty-six rows — six screenfuls past the fold — wearing the
      CREATE role's dashed empty socket. That is right for "append to a list
      you are already reading" and wrong for the only thing on this screen
      that is not "tap a row". It moves under the note and takes the PRIMARY
      face.

      THIS IS THE THIRD SOLID ION SURFACE IN THE APP, and zs-kit §7's census
      has been updated to say so rather than being quietly broken. The RULE
      that census serves is untouched, and is exactly why this qualifies: a
      cyan slab means "press this and something happens", and adding a button
      is that. §11 already made the same move for ＋ New config on the Configs
      screen — same screen shape, same answer.

   2. EVERY ROW LOOKED THE SAME, and they are not the same. A row either
      leaves the button doing itself (it carries the "default" .tag), or it
      has been REMAPPED, or a whole Smart Action rides on it (data-act is then
      not "-1"). Three states, three appearances — rail, ink and weight, never
      colour alone: the "default" chip and the target's NAME already say which
      is which in words, and both stay exactly as they were.

   3. IT SCROLLED AS ONE UNBROKEN SLAB. Sixteen rows and then twenty is not a
      list, it is a wall. Every fourth row takes a stronger divider. That is a
      reading rhythm and it is offered as one — ruled paper, not a claim about
      the buttons — and it is what lets a thumb keep its place. Row order
      comes from PAD_SRC_CODE, so the rhythm is stable.

   The ordering is done with flex `order` because the markup belongs to
   smart-actions.js: nothing here renames a class or moves a node, so that
   renderer goes on drawing exactly what it draws. */

#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
/* `flex: none` is load-bearing, not tidiness: a flex column shrinks its
   children by default, and without it the whole 2703px list was squeezed into
   one 739px viewport and the screen stopped scrolling. */
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > * { order: 0; flex: none; }
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > .lead { order: -3; }
/* the LEADING notes only — this screen also ends with one, and hoisting every
   .note would have carried the footer up to the top with them */
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > .note:nth-child(-n+3) { order: -2; }
/* #sa-addinput used to be hoisted to the top here (order: -1). The owner
   asked for it at the BOTTOM (2026-09-04, "put add a button on the bottom"),
   which is where smart-actions.js emits it — so the rule is gone and the
   markup order stands. */

/* the primary face, on the create control that IS this screen's first move.
   Same cap physics and the same measured pairs as .btn.primary (zs-kit §7):
   --btn-1-ink #04120f on the cap's mid stop 10.87:1, on its foot 8.82:1. */
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > #sa-addinput {
  min-height: 52px;
  border: 1px solid var(--btn-1-edge, #a8c4ff);
  border-radius: var(--r-card, 14px);
  background: var(--btn-1-bg, #6f9bff);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 100%);
  color: var(--btn-1-ink, #04120f);
  font-weight: 700;
  /* explicit, because a flex column does not collapse margins: the note above
     and the header below both stop contributing one, so this row owns its
     whole gap on both sides */
  margin: 14px 0 4px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.42),
              0 3px 0 var(--btn-1-wall, #123a6b),
              0 8px 20px rgba(111, 155, 255,.22);
}
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > #sa-addinput:active,
#dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > #sa-addinput.zs-pressing {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
  border-style: solid;
}

/* THE THREE ROW STATES.
   default  — the button does itself. Quiet: no rail, target name muted.
   remapped — this button has been given another job. Violet rail, name in
              full ink.
   sequence — a Smart Action rides on it. Solid violet rail and the room's
              wash behind the row, so the heaviest-looking row in the list is
              the row that does the most.
     violet #a371f7 measures 5.30:1 on a card; the wash sits at 14%. */
[data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow {
  border-left: 3px solid transparent;
  padding-left: 11px;                       /* 14 − 3, so the ink does not move */
}
[data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:has(.tag) .intn { color: var(--muted); }
[data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:not(:has(.tag)) {
  border-left-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 55%, transparent);
}
/* 700, not 600: measured, .intn was ALREADY 600 in both states, so weight was
   not a channel at all until this line — the difference has to be visible
   with the colour taken away, and now it is (rail, ink and weight, three) */
[data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:not(:has(.tag)) .intn {
  color: var(--text); font-weight: 700;
}
[data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:not([data-act="-1"]) {
  border-left-color: var(--tint);
  background-color: var(--tint-wash);
}
/* the reading rhythm: a stronger cut every fourth row */
[data-tint="bindings"] .grp-card > .lrow.inrow:nth-child(4n+1):not(:first-child) {
  border-top-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint) 34%, transparent);
}
/* the sequence rows in the two groups below the buttons ("Smart Actions",
   "Not attached to a button yet") wear the same rail — zs-kit:2306 pins them
   to the neutral --zs-edge, which is one more room-blind token in a room that
   has a colour */
[data-tint] .lrow.seqline { border-left-color: var(--tint-edge); }


/* ── 18. ACCESSIBILITY, RESTATED FOR §16–17 ────────────────────────────────
   The same trade the rest of this file makes: the washes, veils and gradients
   go; the strong indicators stay — the tint keeps the 3px rail under a
   selected segment and the solid role fills keep their fill.
   Verified with Emulation.setEmulatedMedia rather than by reading, and that
   is how the specificity note inside the first block was found.
   One honest asymmetry, inherited not introduced: under `prefers-contrast:
   more` the card's own defined border (zs-kit §14) outranks §16's tint spine,
   so the spine is a full boundary there rather than a coloured edge; under
   body.a11y-contrast §16's spine survives. Both are legible; neither mode
   loses a channel, because in both the room still speaks through the rail
   under the selection and through the header. */

@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  [data-tint] .grp-card, [data-tint] .acc, [data-tint] .vizbox {
    background-image: none;
  }
  [data-tint] .stp button { background-image: none; border-color: var(--text); }
  /* THE SELECTOR SHAPE IS THE POINT, not the declarations: §16's selection
     rule matches at (0,3,2), so a shorter restatement here LOSES the tie and
     the fill stays solid in high contrast. Measured with
     Emulation.setEmulatedMedia, not read. The extra [data-tint] carries the
     weight now that §16 no longer needs a :not() — one rule for six rooms.
     The 3px rail keeps the ROOM's hue here on purpose: in a mode with no
     washes and no gradients it is the only "which room" signal left. */
  body [data-tint][data-tint] .seg2 button.on,
  body [data-tint][data-tint] .subtabs button.on,
  body [data-tint][data-tint] #edtabs button.on {
    background-color: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
    border: 2px solid var(--text);
    box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--tint);
  }
  [data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:not([data-act="-1"]) { background-color: transparent; }
  #dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > #sa-addinput {
    background-image: none; border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none;
  }
}
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .grp-card,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .acc,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .vizbox { background-image: none; }
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .stp button {
  background-image: none; border-color: var(--text);
}
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint][data-tint] .seg2 button.on,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint][data-tint] .subtabs button.on,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint][data-tint] #edtabs button.on {
  background-color: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
  border: 2px solid var(--text);
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--tint);
}
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint="bindings"] .lrow.inrow:not([data-act="-1"]) {
  background-color: transparent;
}
body.a11y-contrast #dbody[data-tint="bindings"] > #sa-addinput {
  background-image: none; border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
  [data-tint] .grp-card, [data-tint] .acc, [data-tint] .vizbox {
    background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  }
}


/* ── 19. WHICH PLAYSTATION ACCOUNT THIS CONFIG PLAYS AS ────────────────────
   One row inside the Remote Play card, and the loudest fact in it — so it
   carries the card's only semibold left-hand value, the way a door row does.

   IT NEEDS ITS OWN HORIZONTAL PADDING and that is not cosmetic. zs-kit.css
   pads rows with `.grp-card > .stat, .grp-card > .lrow` — a DIRECT-child
   selector. This row is painted into a placeholder div (#ov-rpacct) after the
   card is built, because the account list lives on the board, so it is a
   GRANDCHILD and would have sat flush against both edges while the stat rows
   above and the switch below stayed inset. Restated here rather than by
   flattening the wrapper, because the wrapper is what lets the row be
   repainted without rebuilding the card around it.

   `.none` is the "nothing linked yet / your board cannot name it" state:
   amber, the app's colour for not-set-up, never red — red is spent on the kill
   switch and stays spent (docs/DESIGN.md). The row says it in words as well,
   so the hue is a second channel and never the signal. */
#ov-rpacct > .lrow { padding-left: var(--sp-3, 14px); padding-right: var(--sp-3, 14px); }
#ov-rpacct .lrow .src b { color: var(--text); font-weight: 700; }
#ov-rpacct .lrow.none .src b,
#ov-rpacct .lrow.none .arrow { color: var(--amber); }
/* High contrast drops the hue and keeps the row readable — the words already
   carry the state, so nothing is lost by giving the value the plain ink. */
body.a11y-contrast #ov-rpacct .lrow.none .src b { color: var(--text); }

/* PRESS FEEDBACK, ON POINTER-DOWN, ON THE TWO ROWS THIS FEATURE ADDS.
   zs-motion.js's pressable() delegates on a fixed selector list that includes
   .btn, .card, .item and .drow but NOT .lrow — so no door row in the app has
   ever carried .zs-pressing. That is a real gap and the fix is one word in
   that list, but it would change the feel of every door in the product and
   does not belong inside this feature (docs/DESIGN.md: feedback belongs on
   pointer-down, and "the moment it waits for touch-up, directness falls off a
   cliff"). What is scoped here is the same :active tint zs-kit.css already
   gives `.fld.tapper`, so the two controls added today are not the only
   tappable things on their screens with nothing under the finger. */
#ov-rpacct .lrow.tapper:active,
#ov-rpacct .lrow.tapper.zs-pressing,
.lrow.tapper[data-rpi]:active,
.lrow.tapper[data-rpi].zs-pressing { background-color: var(--press-wash); }


/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   LANE 1 — THE BROKEN THINGS
   Six defects that are not taste: text that hard-clips, a mark drawn twice,
   a toast that lands off the device, and a status strip advertising the
   SoC's ADC channels as if they were controllers.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── 1. THE HEADER CLIPPED INSTEAD OF ELLIPSISING ──────────────────────────
   .pback carries overflow:hidden + text-overflow:ellipsis from phone.html's
   header block, but zs-kit paints it display:inline-flex — and text-overflow
   does not apply to a flex container's ANONYMOUS text. Measured at 380px:
   #dtback clientWidth 96 / scrollWidth 114, rendering "‹ Call of Dut" with
   the y painted across the button's own border and no ellipsis anywhere.
   The label now sits in .pbtx, a real element, which text-overflow can act
   on; the button keeps its flex centring and its 44px target. */
.pback .pbtx {
  display: block;
  min-width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
/* AND IT WAS SHRINKING PAST ITS OWN CAP. phone.html's header note describes
   the intent exactly — "capped at 40% of the header and ellipsises there,
   and the title column takes everything that is left" — but .pback was
   written flex:0 1 auto, so on an over-full row it did not stop at the cap:
   it shrank with the title, all the way down to "‹ Ho…" for a six-character
   label. flex:0 0 auto makes the cap the cap: the key takes min(its own
   width, 36%) and the title column absorbs every pixel of the overflow, which
   is the behaviour the note was already claiming. 36% rather than 40% is the
   13px "Global Settings" was short of (121 available / 130 needed). */
.phead .pback { flex: 0 0 auto; max-width: 31%; padding: 0 10px; }
/* The 18px the title was short of comes out of the two things beside it that
   were spending pixels on air, not on words: the back key's 13px side padding
   (a 44px target needs the height, not the width — the hit box is unchanged)
   and the chip's 9px + .04em tracking. Measured at a 375px phone: the longest
   title in the app, "Smart Actions" at 124px, now has 124px. Nothing shrank
   its type and nothing lost its touch target. */
.phead h2 { letter-spacing: -.005em; }

/* ── 2. THE MARK WAS DRAWN TWICE, AND THE SECOND COPY ESCAPED ──────────────
   `.art.mkart .mkbadge` is position:relative, and `.hero-full .hshot .art
   .mkbadge` sets right:10px/bottom:10px — on a RELATIVE box those offsets
   push the element UP and LEFT out of its parent instead of pinning it to
   the corner. Measured: the badge landed at x34/y302 while the art box
   starts at x43/y312, i.e. 9px left and 10px above its own container, with
   its top edge exactly on the bottom edge of the identical mark already
   drawn in the subtitle line. Two marks, 0px apart, one of them outside the
   card it belongs to. The art tile is the one that goes: the subtitle line's
   mark sits beside the config name, which is where identity belongs. */
.hero-full .hshot .art .mkbadge { display: none; }

/* ── 3. THE TOAST WAS POSITIONED AGAINST THE PHONE FRAME ───────────────────
   Not against the screen. At a 380px viewport it rendered 66px BELOW the
   device's bottom edge — off the phone entirely. At 400px it sat on the
   device bar. At 418px it covered EDIT CONFIG, which is how Caleb found it.
   It is now anchored inside .screen and floated clear of the device bar, so
   it can only ever land over the bottom of the content it is talking about,
   and never over the primary action or off the glass. */
.screen { position: relative; }
/* bottom:24px was measured against the FRAME, which is 100+px taller than the
   glass — so where the toast landed depended on the window: 66px BELOW the
   device at 380px, on the device bar at 400px, over EDIT CONFIG at 418px.
   Anchored to the screen it is deterministic, and it is parked in the
   device-bar band on purpose. Every scroll container in the app already
   reserves --dvb-h of bottom padding for that bar, so a toast that lives
   inside that band CANNOT cover a control — the worst it can do is hide
   ambient status for three seconds, which is the correct thing to sacrifice.
   The slide-in transform is untouched: it belongs to the animation, not the
   anchoring, and overriding it here stopped the toast animating at all. */
.toast { bottom: var(--toast-lift, 14px); z-index: 60; }
/* ...AND THE BAND MOVED ON HOME (2026-09-04). The paragraph above is still
   the rule - park the toast where it cannot cover a control - but HOME's
   bottom band is no longer the device bar: it is the live pad and the mark
   (#hfoot). Neither is a control, so the "cannot cover a control" guarantee
   holds either way; what changed is that the first toast a new owner ever
   sees ("connected - configs loaded") landed square on the product's mark.
   --toast-lift carries the foot's REAL height, published by phone.html's
   syncFootLift() rather than hard-coded, because the pad's drawing decides
   that height and a number typed here would be wrong the first time the art
   changed. Every other screen keeps 14px: the device bar is still what is
   underneath there. */

/* ── 4. THE DEVICE BAR STOPPED SHOUTING KERNEL NAMES ───────────────────────
   See phone.html's devbar renderer: the five chips it dropped were
   sunxi-gpadc0/channel0..3 (the A733's own ADC lines) and axp8191-pek (the
   PMIC's power button). What is left is what a person plugged in. The strip
   strip was also 1009px of content in a 317px row — 69% of it off-screen
   sideways, reachable only by dragging a 32px band at the very bottom edge.
   It stays a scroller (the bar's height is a fixed reserve, --dvb-h, that
   every scroll container pads for, so it must not grow), but what it has to
   scroll is now the things a person actually plugged in. */

/* ── 5. THE OFFLINE MESSAGE WAS UNDER THE OVERLAY ──────────────────────────
   Screen Assist draws its region boxes and reticle in the preview frame at a
   z-index above the frame's own empty/error state, so "Can't reach your
   adapter" was rendered with a green rectangle and an "Area 1 off" pill
   painted across the middle of the sentence. When there is no picture there
   is nothing for a region box to be a region OF, so the overlay stands down
   and the message is readable. */
#cv-stage[data-nopic="1"] #cv-boxes,
#cv-stage[data-nopic="1"] #cv-ovl { display: none; }


/* ══ LANE 3 — THE DEFAULT CONFIG ART, AND THE TEXT DIET ═══════════════════
   Owner, 2026-08-28: a config with no photo "shows a FLAT MAGENTA GRADIENT —
   it looks empty/broken", and he asked for "a proper default".

   MEASURED BEFORE: the hero tile was 326×396 in a 768px screen — 51.6% of
   HOME — and its only child was a family badge that §2 above had just set to
   display:none (correctly: it was a second copy of the mark already drawn
   19px away on the identity line). The engraved-initial layer never fired
   either, because artHTML only drew it for a config with a GAME locked and
   the flagship config is Game = "None". So half of HOME was a bare colour
   wash with literally nothing in it.

   WHAT IT IS NOW — a PLATE: the config's colour as the ground it always was,
   the app's own reticle engraved into the middle of it, and the config's
   monogram sitting in the reticle's ring. Three reasons this composition and
   not another:

   1. It is the app's existing language, not a new one. The reticle geometry
      is lifted from zs-kit.css §5.1 `.hero::after`, which engraves the same
      mark into the card's top-right corner — the same construction, the same
      engraved-black argument ("a darker mark can never hurt legibility on a
      dark card, so it can sit where a lighter one could not").
   2. It is NOT the family badge again. §2 removed that copy for duplicating
      the identity line; putting it back larger would be the same defect at a
      different size. The monogram is per-CONFIG, so it is the one layer that
      distinguishes two PS5 controller configs from each other — which is the
      question the picture exists to answer.
   3. A crosshair centred on the thing it is aimed at is what this product
      does. It reads as artwork on purpose rather than as a missing image.

   IT IS A CLASS, NOT A PLACE. `.plate` is written by artHTML at hero scale
   and on request, so the photo sheet's "no photo" preview can show the tile
   the app actually draws instead of the badge-on-colour square it used to
   hand-roll — one treatment, two surfaces, no third look to keep in sync.

   CONTRAST, measured against all eight palette entries rather than the one
   that happened to be on screen. The tile centre is color-mix(--cfg 55%,
   #0d1118), so the ground the letter sits on runs from rgb(41,109,55) on the
   green config to rgb(131,106,47) on the gold. White at .90 over those
   measures 4.54:1 at worst (gold) and 6.65:1 at best (red) — over AA for
   normal text on every one, and this is a 112px glyph. Everything else on
   the tile is engraved BLACK, which cannot lower the contrast of anything
   drawn over it; that is the same argument zs-kit.css §5.1 makes for the
   corner reticle, and it is why the plate can carry a mark where a lighter
   one would have to be measured against the "Playing now" pill. */
.art.mkart.plate,
.hero-full .hshot .art.mkart {
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
}
/* THE FAMILY BADGE STANDS DOWN ON A PLATE. §2 above removed it from the hero
   because it was a second copy of the mark already drawn on the identity line
   19px away; the plate is the treatment that reasoning belongs to, so the
   rule moves onto the class and the photo sheet inherits the same call. The
   badge is untouched at row scale, where it is the only family cue there is
   and no identity line is repeating it. */
.art.mkart.plate .mkbadge { display: none; }
/* THE ENGRAVED RETICLE. Percentage geometry throughout, so the one absolute
   in it — the 2px tick — stays a hairline as the plate grows instead of
   thickening with it. aspect-ratio keeps it a circle inside a tile that is
   taller than it is wide.

   `closest-side` IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the trap zs-kit.css §5.1 fell
   into. A radial-gradient's percentages are fractions of the GRADIENT RAY,
   and the default ray runs to the farthest CORNER — so the kit's "43%" ring
   in a 172px box draws at 43% of 122px, i.e. a circle a third the width of
   the element it is centred in. Measured here at the hero: a 215.6px plate
   drew a 131px ring, which the monogram then had to fight its way out of.
   closest-side makes 100% the half-width, so these numbers mean what they
   read as: the ring sits at 74% of the way to the edge and the four ticks
   live in the 24% of clear ground outside it. */
.art.mkart.plate::before,
.hero-full .hshot .art.mkart::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: 0; pointer-events: none;
  left: 50%; top: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  width: min(66%, 250px); aspect-ratio: 1;
  --ret: rgba(0,0,0,.34);
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(circle closest-side at 50% 50%,
      transparent 0 74%, var(--ret) 74% 76.4%, transparent 76.9%),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret));
  background-size: 100% 100%, 2px 8%, 2px 8%, 8% 2px, 8% 2px;
  background-position: 0 0, 50% 1%, 50% 99%, 1% 50%, 99% 50%;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}
/* THE MONOGRAM. `.art.mkart i.gm0` in phone.html's floor block is (0,3,1)
   and pins the letter to the bottom-right corner as a texture; five classes
   here is (0,5,0), which outranks it on the class count — deliberately, and
   for the same reason that floor rule outranks the kit: at hero scale this
   letter is the subject, not the wallpaper. */
.art.mkart.plate .gm0-plate,
.hero-full .hshot .art.mkart .gm0-plate {
  position: relative; z-index: 1;
  right: auto; bottom: auto;
  /* the hero only — every other plate is a known box, and artHTML writes its
     44% inline, which wins over this */
  font-size: min(27vw, 112px);
  line-height: 1;
  color: rgba(255,255,255,.90);
  text-shadow: 0 2px 14px rgba(0,0,0,.55);
}
/* High-contrast mode already flattens .art.mkart's gradient and hides .gm0
   (zs-kit.css §a11y). The reticle is texture by the same argument, so it
   goes with them rather than becoming the only thing left on a flat tile. */
body.a11y-contrast .art.mkart.plate::before,
body.a11y-contrast .hero-full .hshot .art.mkart::before { display: none; }
/* …and with the reticle and the letter both gone in that mode, the family
   badge is the only identity the plate has left, so it comes back. */
body.a11y-contrast .art.mkart.plate .mkbadge { display: revert; }


/* ── A SHEET PUTS ITS PRIMARY ACTION EXACTLY WHERE THE TOAST PARKS ─────────
   §3 above moved the toast inside .screen and parked it in the device-bar
   band at bottom:14px, which is right for every screen — the scroll
   containers all reserve that band, so it can no longer land on a control.
   A SHEET does not: it is bottom-anchored and its own primary button is the
   last thing in it, so "Take a photo or choose one" sat at y848 and the
   toast at y849-914 covered it. Measured on the config-photo sheet at a
   400px phone, with the toast's z-index 60 over the sheet's 45.

   While a sheet is open the top of the screen is SCRIM — the header behind
   it is inert, and a tap up there dismisses the sheet — so that is the one
   region of the screen where a floating message costs nothing. The toast
   goes there for exactly as long as a sheet is open.

   :has() is already load-bearing in this app (`#home:has(.hero-full)` builds
   the whole home column), so this adds no new engine requirement; where it
   is unsupported the rule drops and the toast keeps today's position.

   VERIFIED BY MEASUREMENT on the two extremes: the short config-photo sheet
   (toast y103-168, its "Take a photo or choose one" button y783-835) and the
   near-full-height sequence editor, where every focusable control in the
   open sheet was tested against the toast's box and NONE intersected it. On
   a tall sheet the toast does land on the sheet's TITLE for the two seconds
   it lives — a word, not a control, and the sheet's ✕ sits clear of it to
   the right. That is the trade this rule makes on purpose. */
.screen:has(#sheetwrap.open) .toast { top: 12px; bottom: auto; }


/* ── ONE RETICLE PER CARD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Reviewer, 2026-08-28. zs-kit.css §5.1 engraves the logo's reticle into the
   hero card's TOP-RIGHT CORNER as a watermark (.hero::after, 172px, pulled
   -34/-30 so it is clipped by the card's radius). The new default art tile
   then draws the SAME reticle, at full size, centred, 20px below it — inside
   that very card.

   Two reticles, and the corner one only ever shows the lower-left quarter of
   its ring plus its little inner circle, so on HOME it reads as a stray arc
   and a floating dot broken across the title band rather than as a
   watermark. Measured at 375px: the plate's ring is 143px centred at y470;
   the watermark's arc crosses the title band at y237-320, one card, two
   circles, neither explained by the other.

   The watermark exists to put the mark on a card that has no other identity.
   A card whose picture IS the mark does not need it, so it stands down there
   and stays everywhere else. Scoped to :has(.plate) so a hero with a real
   photo — which has no reticle of its own — keeps the watermark it was
   designed for. */
.hero:has(.art.plate)::after { display: none; }


/* ── D121. THE CONFIGS LIST: A NAME, A PICTURE AND A SEARCH ───────────────
   The owner, 2026-09-23: "when you are looking through the configs it
   should just say the config name, it should also have the picture of the
   config ... make sure there is a search bar to search configs."

   THE ROW. The identity line under the name (console · way to play · game)
   is gone, and its width went to the picture: 111 x 52, the shape of the
   game's own header (§3 above). The name may now take two lines, since
   nothing else competes for the column, so "PS5 - Mouse & Keyboard" reads
   whole beside a picture instead of ellipsing after "PS5 - Mou". The
   PLAYING pill (§1) is unchanged: the tick and the word under the name. */
.cfg .cn .name {
  white-space: normal;
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  overflow: hidden;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
/* A whole picture (.fit) wears its config-colour stripe as a 4px border
   UNDER the picture (phone.html), so it stands 56px tall; our own composed
   tile, whose stripe is drawn inside it, is made the same 56px so every row
   lines up. */
.cfg .art.mkart { height: 56px !important; }
/* Bigger touch targets: a bigger picture too, same shape. */
body.a11y-big .cfg .art { width: 128px !important; height: 60px !important; }
body.a11y-big .cfg .art.mkart { height: 64px !important; }
/* A row the search hides. The row is display:flex, which out-ranks the
   browser's own [hidden] rule, so hidden is said here as well (the script
   also sets it inline, for a page that lost this sheet). */
.cfg[hidden], .cfgnone[hidden] { display: none !important; }
/* ...and a game tile the wizard's search hides (D125), for the same reason */
.wz-glist .tile[hidden] { display: none !important; }

/* THE SEARCH FIELD. The app's own text-field well (input.txt, zs-kit §9.4:
   "you type INTO these, so they are wells, not keys"), with the search
   picture inside its left edge and a real 44px clear key inside its right
   one. 16px text, because iOS zooms the whole page into a field whose text
   is smaller. It sits directly on top of the rows it filters, so the
   control is next to what it changes. No motion: nothing here moves. */
.cfgfind { position: relative; margin: 10px 0 2px; }
.cfgfind .cfgfind-ico {
  position: absolute; left: 13px; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%);
  width: 20px; height: 20px; pointer-events: none; color: var(--muted);
}
.cfgfind input.txt {
  width: 100%; min-height: 48px;
  padding-left: 42px; padding-right: 46px;
  font-size: 16px;
  -webkit-appearance: none; appearance: none;
}
.cfgfind input.txt::placeholder { color: var(--muted); opacity: 1; }
/* the browser's own little clear cross: ours is the 44px one */
.cfgfind input::-webkit-search-cancel-button,
.cfgfind input::-webkit-search-decoration { -webkit-appearance: none; appearance: none; display: none; }
.cfgfind .cfgfind-x {
  position: absolute; right: 2px; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%);
  width: 44px; height: 44px; padding: 0;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  border: 0; border-radius: 999px; background: none;
  color: var(--muted); font: inherit; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.cfgfind .cfgfind-x:active { color: var(--accent); }
.cfgfind .cfgfind-x[hidden] { display: none; }
/* the honest empty state: the words, in the note's own voice */
.cfgnone { margin-top: 10px; }

/* THE WIZARD'S GAME TILES carry the game's wide picture over their words
   (phone-sections.js gameGridHtml), whole and at the header's own shape.
   No config colour stripe: a game on offer is not a config yet. */
.wz-glist .tile .gpic {
  display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 460 / 215;
  border-radius: 10px; margin: 0 0 8px;
}
.wz-glist .tile .gpic::after { display: none; }
